<p>Something more wonderful has been happening for the last couple of days: the number of people who reached me by email reached (or is about to reach) double digits for the first time since&hellip; well, since I started having a blog, I think. Oh, and I say &ldquo;more wonderful&rdquo; because getting the communication I&rsquo;m already getting is wonderful.</p>
<p>I always had folks reaching out, though usually through Mastodon or Micro.blog (which is kind of Mastodon also, in a way) and when I wrote about <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Emacs">Emacs</a> or org-mode. This time it&rsquo;s just other bloggers and readers saying hello.</p>
<p>It seems most of them were motivated by my post about, well, <a href="https://taonaw.com/2026/04/22/about-writing-other-bloggers-email.html">writing other bloggers emails</a>&hellip; can&rsquo;t get more to the point than that!</p>
<p>I&rsquo;m going through the careful process of reading each email fast (to get a feel for it), then seeing if the person who sent it has a website (most of the time, they do), then researching this website to see if I&rsquo;m adding them to my RSS feed (Inkwell is handling a lot of testing recently), then reading the email again more thoroughly before I answer, which I always do.</p>
<p>It&rsquo;s a pleasure to read each email. It really is.</p>
<p>Of course, it gives an immediate ego boost when someone takes some time of their day to actually write an email to me (I guess my online ramblings are not <em>that</em> bad after all, says the voice in my head). But besides that, it&rsquo;s always like opening a box of chocolates: you never know what you&rsquo;re going to get (cue up the Forrest Gump theme). Each person is unique; each email brings in another whole <em>brain</em> to follow up on, read about, learn from, and talk to. I love it.</p>
<p>If you&rsquo;re one of these folks who reached out, thank you! I&rsquo;ll get back to you as soon as my above process allows (and work. And life after work. And after I get some groceries that are aching to be in my fridge. And OK, I also need to exercise. But I&rsquo;ll get there).</p>
<p>It also makes me want to write more. Not just to new folks I keep finding and adding, but also to those I follow already - either because something they wrote makes me think or because I&rsquo;m just wondering what&rsquo;s going on.</p>
<p>Well alrightie then! Let&rsquo;s start writing some emails.</p>
